#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# profile

#% text
# `profile(e)` evaluates `e` and gives `[seconds, value, rows]`: the wall-clock time, the value, and where the time went, one row `[f, kind, calls, own, total]` per function.

#% text
# `profile(e, top: n)` keeps the n rows with the most own time.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `kind` is `"library"` for functions built into the system, `"definitions"` for functions defined by rules (`fn f(x) = ...`), and `"compiled"` for compiled code (`compile(f)`).

#% item
# `calls` counts the calls that ran (values the evaluator remembered from earlier evaluations are not recomputed and not counted); `total` is their time in seconds including the calls they made; `own` is the part not spent in other measured calls.

#% item
# The rows are sorted by own time, largest first; 25 are kept by default. The last row, `[evaluation, "interpreter", 1, own, total]`, is the time outside every measured call.

#% item
# In a parallel evaluation (`parallel_table`, `map` of expensive calls) the times are added over the worker threads, so they may exceed the wall-clock time, and the function that split the work counts its waiting as its own time.

#% item
# Profiling adds a little time to every call. Profiles do not nest: an inner `profile` gives no rows of its own.

#% item
# `timing(e, breakdown: true)` gives a summary of the same measurements.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The value is the second element, the rows the third:

#% cell
fn f(x) = n_integrate(sin(x*t)^2, [t, 0, 1])
p = profile(map(f, [1, 2, 3]))
p[2]
#% output
#= [0.27267564329357963, 0.5946003119134912, 0.5232846248499106]

#% text
# The functions called, with their kinds and call counts:

#% cell
map(r => r[[1, 2, 3]], select(p[3], r => member([f, map, n_integrate], r[1])))
#% output
#= [[n_integrate, "library", 3], [map, "library", 1], [f, "definitions", 3]]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# Only the largest row and the interpreter's:

#% cell
length(profile(map(f, [4, 5]), top: 1)[3])
#% output
#= 2

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Finding the slow part of a computation: the rows with the most own time come first.

#% cell
fn g(x) = factor(x^24 - 1)
q = profile(map(g, [x, y]))
q[3][1][1] == factor
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The value is what the expression gives without `profile`:

#% cell
profile(expand((a + b)^3))[2] === expand((a + b)^3)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# A caller's total time covers its callees':

#% cell
r = profile(map(f, [6, 7]))[3]
select(r, s => s[1] == f)[1][5] >= select(r, s => s[1] == n_integrate)[1][5]
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `timing`, `parallel_table`, `compile`
